WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.965
Reading public benchmarks and leaderboards critically.

2
00:00:03.245 --> 00:00:06.403
Public results are useful for deciding what to test.

3
00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:08.496
They rarely decide what to use.

4
00:00:08.776 --> 00:00:15.176
Studio 1 introduced the main sources; here are the five failure modes to check, with the evidence for each.

5
00:00:15.456 --> 00:00:16.629
Contamination.

6
00:00:16.909 --> 00:00:19.384
Public test items leak into training data.

7
00:00:19.664 --> 00:00:27.621
Deng and colleagues (2023) found models could guess masked answer options on common benchmarks far above chance.

8
00:00:27.901 --> 00:00:45.544
Zhang and colleagues (2024) wrote GSM1k, a fresh set matched in style and difficulty to the GSM8k math benchmark, and found some models scored up to 8 points lower on it, with signs of systematic overfitting in several model families.

9
00:00:45.824 --> 00:00:49.344
They also found frontier models showed minimal overfitting.

10
00:00:49.624 --> 00:00:56.387
Contamination is real and uneven, so a fresh, private test set is worth more than an old public one.

11
00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:57.712
Saturation.

12
00:00:57.992 --> 00:01:07.656
Once top models score above 90 percent, the remaining differences are a handful of questions, and lesson 7’s intervals say a handful of questions isn’t much.

13
00:01:07.936 --> 00:01:19.008
The Humanity’s Last Exam paper (Phan and colleagues, 2025) was motivated by exactly this, noting that models exceeded 90 percent on MMLU.

14
00:01:19.288 --> 00:01:28.376
Anthropic’s agent eval guide describes SWE-bench Verified rising from roughly 40 percent to over 80 percent in about a year.

15
00:01:28.656 --> 00:01:33.904
A saturated benchmark tells you a model is competent; it can’t rank competent models.
